\description{
\code{est_dml()} implements the double/debiased machine learning (DML) recipe of
Chernozhukov et al. (2018): a Neyman-orthogonal score, cross-fitted
nuisance predictions, and influence-function inference. Two models are
available.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{model = "plr"}, the partially linear regression
\code{Y = theta * D + g(X) + e}, estimated by the partialling-out score
(Robinson 1988): residualize \code{Y} and \code{D} on \code{X}, then regress the
residuals on each other. \code{D} may be continuous or binary.
\item \code{model = "irm"}, the interactive regression model with a binary \code{D} and
fully heterogeneous effects, estimated by the doubly robust (AIPW) score
for the ATE or the ATT. This is \code{\link[=est_aipw]{est_aipw()}} with a choice of fold
solution and repeated cross-fitting added.
\item \code{model = "pliv"}, the partially linear instrumental variable model
\verb{Y = theta D + g(X) + e} with \verb{E[e Z | X] = 0}: residualize \code{Y}, \code{D}, and
the instrument(s) \code{Z} on \code{X}, then run instrumental variables on the
residuals (score \code{(Y - l)(Z - m_z) - theta (D - m)(Z - m_z)}). With
several instruments the residualized treatment is projected on the
residualized instruments first (two-stage least squares on residuals).
\item \code{model = "iivm"}, the interactive instrumental variable model with a
binary \code{D} and a binary \code{Z}: the local average treatment effect (LATE)
as the ratio of two AIPW-type scores, the effect of \code{Z} on \code{Y} over the
effect of \code{Z} on \code{D}, with the instrument propensity \code{P(Z = 1 | X)} in
the weights. Each score is doubly robust. The object also reports the
complier share and the intention-to-treat effect.
}

The function owns the causal-estimation layer only. Nuisance predictions
come either from \code{mlr3} learners fitted internally with cross-fitting, or
from outside (any R package, Python, a CSV) through the \verb{*_hat} arguments,
which must then be out-of-fold predictions.

\strong{Scores.} Both scores are linear in \code{theta}, \code{psi = psi_b - psi_a * theta}, so \code{theta_hat = sum(psi_b) / sum(psi_a)} and the standard error is
\code{sqrt(sum(psi_hat^2) / (n - 1)) / (abs(mean(psi_a)) * sqrt(n))} with
\code{psi_hat} evaluated at \code{theta_hat}. For the partially linear model
\code{psi_a = (D - m(X))^2} and \code{psi_b = (Y - l(X)) (D - m(X))}; the standard
error equals the HC1 sandwich of the residual-on-residual regression. For
the interactive model the ATE score is the AIPW score (\code{psi_a = 1}) and the
ATT score is \verb{psi_b = (D - (1 - D) p / (1 - p)) (Y - mu0) / mean(D)},
\code{psi_a = D / mean(D)}.

\strong{Cross-fitting.} With \code{folds = K}, each nuisance is fitted on \code{K - 1}
folds and predicted on the held-out fold, so every observation's nuisance
prediction comes from a model that never saw it. The pooled solution
solves the stacked score once; the fold-average solution averages the
fold-specific solutions. Both use the same variance estimator.

\strong{Repeated cross-fitting.} With \code{n_rep = S}, the whole procedure is
repeated on \code{S} independent partitions. The reported estimate is the median
of the \code{S} estimates and the reported variance is the median of
\code{se_s^2 + (theta_s - theta_median)^2}, which accounts for the variability
across partitions. The \code{score}, \code{residuals}, and \code{nuisance} components refer
to the repetition whose estimate is closest to the median
(\code{rep_selected}).

\strong{Diagnostics.} \code{diagnostics$nuisance} reports the cross-fitted RMSE and
an R-squared-type fit for each nuisance; large RMSE relative to the
outcome's scale means the score is doing little debiasing.
\code{diagnostics$identification} (partially linear model) reports the mean
squared treatment residual \code{mean((D - m(X))^2)}, the denominator of the
estimator: when it is small relative to \code{var(D)}, the covariates predict
the treatment almost perfectly and the coefficient is weakly identified.
For the interactive model the propensity, weight, and effective sample size
summaries of \code{\link[=est_aipw]{est_aipw()}} are reported.
}
